-- title:
--   Clearing denominators in the subalgebra R[I/a]⊆ S
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $S$ a commutative $R$-algebra, $I\subseteq R$ an ideal and $a\in I$. Consider the subset of $S$ consisting of those $y$ for which there exists $i\in I$ with $y\cdot\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,S}(a)=\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,S}(i)$, i.e. those elements of $S$ whose product with the image of $a$ lies in the image of $I$; let $x$ be an element of the $R$-subalgebra of $S$ generated by this subset. The conclusion is that there exist a natural number $n$ and an element $c\in I^n$ with $x\cdot\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,S}(a)^n=\mathrm{algebraMap}_{R,S}(c)$ in $S$. Thus every element of the subalgebra $R[I/a]\subseteq S$ can be written, after multiplication by a power of (the image of) $a$, as the image of an element of the corresponding power of $I$. No hypothesis is imposed making $a$ a non-zero-divisor in $S$, so the exponent $n$ and the element $c$ are asserted to exist rather than to be determined.
--
--   This is the Rees-algebra description of the affine $a$-chart of the blow-up of $\operatorname{Spec} R$ along $I$: the chart algebra is $\sum_n I^n a^{-n}$, and the statement is the 'clearing denominators' form of that description inside an arbitrary $R$-algebra $S$. It is used in the construction of the blown-up charts of modular curves at full level, where elements of a chart algebra must be exhibited with an explicit denominator $a^n$ and numerator in $I^n$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Algebra_exists_pow_mul_eq_of_mem_adjoin_div.lean

